{{Politics of Balisca}}

The '''Low Impact Initiative''' ({{lang-bl|Iniciativa Baixo Impacto|italics=on}}, '''IBI'''), is a {{W|popular referendum|popular initiative}} that has put forward a comprehensive set of urban planning, transportation, and ecological development policies, first proposed in Balisca in 1977 during the onset of the country's [[Special Period]]. Developed in response to acute fuel shortages, economic crisis, and wartime disruption, the initiative marked a decisive shift away from [[W:Fossil fuel dependency|fossil fuel dependency]] and [[W:Car dependency|car-centric development]]. Rooted in principles of sustainability, self-sufficiency, and communal ownership, the initiative has substantially reshaped Baliscan cities and mobility around low-emission, human-centered infrastructure.

The IBI is widely credited with laying the foundation for Balisca’s enduring commitment to eco-socialist urbanism, electrified public transport, and equitable access to space and services ("[[W:Right to the city|right to the city]]"). Over the following decades, it has evolved into a hallmark of Baliscan urban-planning policy and identity, influencing housing design, [[W:Intermodal passenger transport|intermodal transport]], and [[Transición energética|energy infrastructure]] across the archipelago.

== Background and impact ==
The initiative was first proposed in response to the onset of the [[Special Period]]. Prior to the Special Period, rising economic prosperity had led to increasing car ownership among urban Baliscan households. However, the oil shock and fuel shortages following the [[Camboriú War|outbreak of war with Potocsí]] and the simultaneous [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis|Ayori attack on Sanyan]] (which significantly interrupted oil imports) swiftly reversed this trend. With fuel prices skyrocketing and the domestic supply rationed and prioritized for freight rail and emergency services, private car use rapidly cratered.  Rather than attempt to revive mass [[W:Car dependency|automobile dependency]], the confederal government and communes seized this moment to pivot decisively away from car-centric planning. Through the efforts of ecologist confederal delegates like [[Otávia Brito]] and [[Diego Navarro]], the automobile came to be seen as a symbol of waste, inequality, and ecological harm. Policies were enacted to repurpose road infrastructure, and encourage alternative forms of mobility. Domestic automobile manufacturers gradually shifted their production lines to produce bicycles, trams, and there was a [[Vidal|renewed interest]] in [[W:Electric vehicle|electric vehicle]] technology.

With fossil fuels tightly rationed, Balisca turned toward electrification and innovation in small-scale transportation. Early experiments with electric bicycles and trams in the late 1970s laid the foundation for the development of locally produced [[W:Micromobility#Light electric vehicles|electric microvehicles]] (EMVs). Rather than emulate large-scale automobile industries abroad, Balisca embraced modular, communal EV programs suited for dense urban environments. The Confederal Electric Mobility Initiative (IMEC), founded in 1980, encouraged collaboration between universities, worker cooperatives, and engineers. Innovation was driven by open-source principles and state grant-supported research.

The crises of the late 1970s catalyzed a radical rethinking of urban form and function in Balisca. Citizens, facing systemic breakdowns in provisioning, demanded greater control over their environments. Communes responded by implementing planning policies grounded in the principles of spatial justice, participatory democracy, and [[W:Environmental sustainability|environmental sustainability]]. Urban renewal efforts emphasized [[W:Mixed-use zoning|mixed-use zoning]], self-sufficient neighborhoods, and pedestrianized infrastructure. Existing [[SSC Cruises|squatter movements]] were legalized and gradually integrated into official planning, turning informal settlements into experimental zones for sustainable development. Public squares, markets, and co-housing initiatives became central features of a human-centric urbanism. This was reflected in the cooperation of communal/cantonal and confederal authorities to ensure that no citizen was more than 500 meters from a public transit access point. 

The shift was also cultural: riding public transport became a symbol of solidarity and resilience, and accessibility, frequency, and affordability were prioritized over profit.

